    "['location slot [parameter1] engine [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service location slot 1', 'parent_command': ['service internal'], 'root': 'service internal', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For the given HUAWEI command `location slot 1 engine 0`, the equivalent Cisco command would be to specify the slot/engine context for NAT or service processing. However, Cisco does not have a direct 1:1 equivalent, as their architecture differs. The closest conceptual match would be defining a service context or specifying a module location (though Cisco typically doesn\\'t use \"engine\" terminology).\\n\\nThe closest Cisco-style equivalent (assuming a Catalyst 6500/7600 with ESP or similar) would be:\\n\\n##\\nservice location slot 1\\nservice context 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: This is an approximate translation since Cisco and Huawei handle service locations differently. In Cisco, such granular slot/engine specifications are typically handled implicitly or through chassis-specific commands (e.g., `hw-module` for ISR G2).', 'trans_template': 'service location slot 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service context 0', 'parent_command': ['service internal'], 'root': 'service internal', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For the given HUAWEI command `location slot 1 engine 0`, the equivalent Cisco command would be to specify the slot/engine context for NAT or service processing. However, Cisco does not have a direct 1:1 equivalent, as their architecture differs. The closest conceptual match would be defining a service context or specifying a module location (though Cisco typically doesn\\'t use \"engine\" terminology).\\n\\nThe closest Cisco-style equivalent (assuming a Catalyst 6500/7600 with ESP or similar) would be:\\n\\n##\\nservice location slot 1\\nservice context 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: This is an approximate translation since Cisco and Huawei handle service locations differently. In Cisco, such granular slot/engine specifications are typically handl